ympic Stadium, Olympic Park, Stratford, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/302463"} "41 from 28 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Defending champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/106480"} "Barbora Špotáková"] " of the Czech Republic was one of the strongest favorites in athletics at the London Olympics. Her biggest challenge was expected to come from Russian " [:a {:href "/athletes/115130"} "Mariya Abakumova"] ", the 2011 World Champion and silver medalist from Beijing. That didn’t happen."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Špotáková was drawn first in the throwing order for the final and with her first mark of 66.90, she effectively won the gold medal, as nobody else would come close to that mark. Abakumova struggled and did not make the final three throws, eventually placing 10th. Špotáková’s second and third rounds of 66.88 and 66.24 would also have brought her gold, but she got her best mark in round four with 69.55, easily winning the title, over four metres ahead of Germans " [:a {:href "/athletes/106477"} "Christina Obergföll"] " (65.16), who had won bronze in 2008, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/125813"} "Linda Stahl"] " (64.91). South African " [:a {:href "/athletes/106498"} "Sunette Viljoen"] ", who led the world list coming into London, placed fourth. Špotáková became only the second woman to defend a javelin title, after East German " [:a {:href "/athletes/69656"} "Ruth Fuchs"] " in 1972-76."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "1544"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/106480"} "Barbora Špotáková"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CZE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CZE.png"}] "CZE"]] [:td {} "66.19 (1)"] [:t